For the description of artworks for datasets, I use the standard cataloguing scheme (especially for paintings) that is used in museums and private collections and is based on systems such as Goskatalog and KAMIS.

This scheme includes:


Artist's name
Title of the work
Inventory number
Date
Materials
Dimensions
Provenance
Condition (state of preservation)
Comments (subject matter, etc.)

This scheme also has certain flaws. Firstly, there is the uncertainty of attribution (the artist's name and the date of creation). Nowadays, it is preferable not to label a work as "by an unknown master"; instead, researchers usually tentatively attribute it to a possible artist, marking the name with a question mark (?). The same applies to dates: it is a rare situation when the exact year of creation is known. Therefore, the only option is to define a chronological framework or at least the century. Secondly, the provenance can be unclear, which sparks debates regarding the origin and content of the work.

Since this is a single-artist collection, there is no need to indicate the author's name in every entry. In the provenance field (as it describes the first stage of the artwork's existence), the city where the work was created will be indicated.

Usually, an inventory number includes various letters and numbers, which depend on the provenance of the works in the collection (private collections, acquisitions, or assignment to a specific department).

In this collection, all works will be designated according to three parameters:

Date: each year will be designated by a letter from A (2000) to AA (2026) according to its position in the alphabet. Once the letters run out, the sequence repeats from the beginning, but with each element doubled (e.g., AA, BB). This is necessary in case any new works from this year are discovered.
Collection type: G for graphics and P for paintings.
Sequential number.
